# Frontend configuration. Copy to .env.local for local work; .env* is gitignored.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where `npm run dev` proxies /api. Set this when the backend does not run on
# localhost:3000 — a different port, a container, or a teammate's machine.
#
# It has no VITE_ prefix on purpose. Only VITE_* variables reach the browser,
# and this one is for the dev server alone.
#DEV_API_TARGET=http://localhost:3000

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Static-host builds only
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave this UNSET for local work and for the Docker image. Both proxy /api to
# the backend — Vite in development, nginx in the image — so the app stays on
# one origin and calls the default '/api'.
#
# Set it only when you deploy dist/ to a static host, where nothing proxies and
# the API needs its absolute URL. Vite inlines the value at BUILD time, so that
# deployment is built for one API and cannot be repointed without rebuilding.
#
# Know the cost before you choose this path. Leaving one origin means:
#
#   * CORS becomes load-bearing. WEB_ORIGIN on the API must match this app's
#     origin exactly — scheme, host and port.
#   * The refresh cookie is SameSite=Lax (backend/src/modules/auth/
#     auth.controller.ts). Lax is sent between different ports or subdomains of
#     ONE registrable domain, so api.example.com and app.example.com are fine.
#     It is NOT sent between genuinely different sites, so a frontend on
#     example.pages.dev with an API on example.com silently fails to refresh —
#     the user is signed out when the access token expires, with no error that
#     points at the cause. That combination needs SameSite=None and Secure,
#     which needs real HTTPS on both.
#
#VITE_API_BASE=https://api.ilham.example
